**Theorem 4.2 (sharp live block dimension three).** For every base, height
and preparation in Definition 4.1, the minimum $d$ in (QC.20)–(QC.21)
is exactly three, even though $n$ may be any finite integer. Four actual
original-control endpoints already force the lower bound. This is a
minimum for the exact live quantum block, under the unassisted coding
contract, and not a minimum for the full observer or its archive.

Proof. First specify the actual finite witness, rather than arbitrary
qutrit states. Put

$$
p=\sqrt Z e_2,
\quad v_+=\sqrt{2k}U_++\sqrt{2(c-k)}U_-,
\quad v_-=\sqrt{2k}U_+-\sqrt{2(c-k)}U_-.
\tag{QC.22}
$$

Select the four states

$$
\phi_1=p+v_+,\quad \phi_2=p-v_+,
\quad \phi_3=p-i v_-,\quad \phi_4=p+i v_-.
\tag{QC.23}
$$

Their $(\beta,m)$ values are respectively
$(0,0),(\pi,0),(\pi/2,1),(3\pi/2,1)$.
For explicit reachability fix one auxiliary $a\in(0,c)$, $a\ne k$,
before selecting a state. Prepend the unit-time fixed-height phase leg of
increment $2\pi m$. Then use the supplied four-leg rectangle from $k$
to $a$ with signed phase height

$$
B_{\beta,m}
  =\frac{r(\pi m-\beta)-2\pi mk}{a-k}.
\tag{QC.24}
$$

The actual residual is

$$
2\pi m w(k)+B_{\beta,m}(w(a)-w(k))
 =(\beta,\beta-\pi m,0,\beta-\pi m,\beta).
\tag{QC.25}
$$

Indeed $w(a)-w(k)=-(a-k)(1,1,0,1,1)/r$; substituting (QC.24)
gives each coordinate. The rectangle's phase increment is zero, so the
full real increment is exactly $2\pi m$. The paths all have duration five
and the same probability waveform: hold $k$, move to $a$, hold $a$,
return to $k$, hold $k$, with one unit per leg. Their finite phase
variations are $2\pi|m|+2|B_{\beta,m}|$. Every probability is at least

$$
\delta=\min\{k,a,c-k,c-a,Z\}>0.
\tag{QC.26}
$$

The affine controls belong to both supplied regularity alternatives and
start from the same preparation. If their preparation is itself made with
the canonical stopped instrument, the successful histories have the same
strictly positive probability: (QC.7) depends only on their common $k$
waveform. Conditioned on the live event, they yield the four exact states
with the same no-failure record and clock. No failed branch is retried.
The prior generator's state or waveform is not handed to the code as
additional information. These are mathematical ordinary-operation
witnesses, not claims of a previously recorded physical occurrence.

Use the inner product conjugate linear in the first argument. Since
$\langle v_+,v_-\rangle=4k-2c$, every cross-parity overlap between
$\{\phi_1,\phi_2\}$ and $\{\phi_3,\phi_4\}$ is one of

$$
Z+i(4k-2c),\qquad Z-i(4k-2c).
\tag{QC.27}
$$

All four are nonzero because $Z>0$. The overlap graph therefore contains
the connected complete bipartite graph on these two pairs. Same-parity
overlaps may vanish; no assumption that every pair overlaps is needed.
Moreover,

$$
\phi_1+\phi_2=2p,
\quad \phi_1-\phi_2=2v_+,
\quad \phi_4-\phi_3=2i v_-.
\tag{QC.28}
$$

Strict $k>0$ and $c-k>0$ make $v_+,v_-$ independent. Since $Z>0$,
these equations show that the four actual vectors span all of $V$.

Now apply the mature pure-state no-imprinting argument to the common
composite channel $\mathcal T=\mathcal R\mathcal C$. For clarity its
exact use is included here. A Stinespring isometry $S$ for $\mathcal T$
has, by (QC.21) and purity,

$$
S\phi_j=\phi_j\otimes\eta_j,\qquad \|\eta_j\|=1.
\tag{QC.29}
$$

Any output phase can be absorbed in $\eta_j$. Isometry gives

$$
\langle\phi_j,\phi_l\rangle
 =\langle\phi_j,\phi_l\rangle\langle\eta_j,\eta_l\rangle.
\tag{QC.30}
$$

On each nonzero-overlap edge (QC.30) forces inner product one, hence equal
environment vectors. Connectivity in (QC.27) gives one common $\eta$.
Linearity and (QC.28) imply $Sx=x\otimes\eta$ for every $x\in V$.
Consequently

$$
\mathcal T(X)=X\qquad(X\in M_3).
\tag{QC.31}
$$

There is no assumption that arbitrary superpositions of the four histories
are preparable. The all-operator identity follows from one linear channel
that exactly fixes their actual pure states.

Write $\mathcal C_\alpha:M_3\to M_{d_\alpha}$ for an encoder branch
and $\mathcal R_\alpha:M_{d_\alpha}\to M_3$ for the decoder on that
block. Each $\mathcal C_\alpha$ is CP and generally trace decreasing;
only their direct sum is TP. Each block decoder is CPTP. Choose finite
Kraus families $C_{\alpha u}$ and $R_{\alpha v}$. Their products give
a Kraus representation of $\mathcal T$ with

$$
Q_{\alpha vu}=R_{\alpha v}C_{\alpha u},
\qquad \operatorname{rank}Q_{\alpha vu}\leq d_\alpha\leq d.
\tag{QC.32}
$$

The Choi matrix of (QC.31), using the unnormalized maximally entangled
vector $\Omega=\sum_{j=1}^3 g_j\otimes g_j$, is

$$
|\Omega\rangle\langle\Omega|
  =\sum_{\alpha,v,u}|Q_{\alpha vu}\rangle\!\rangle
                    \langle\!\langle Q_{\alpha vu}|.
\tag{QC.33}
$$

A sum of positive rank-one matrices with one-dimensional range can have
no summand outside that range: evaluate on every vector orthogonal to
$\Omega$ and use nonnegativity of each summand. Thus every nonzero
$Q_{\alpha vu}$ is a scalar multiple of $I_3$ and has rank three.
At least one is nonzero because $\mathcal T$ is TP. Equation (QC.32)
therefore forces $d\geq3$, for every finite $n$.

For attainment retain the qutrit with coordinates
$(U_+,U_-,e_2)$, use the first three columns of $W$ and its inverse, and
take $n=1,d=3$. The usual whole-domain completion sends the inactive
orthogonal complement to a fixed live state. It is CPTP and exactly
recovers every supported joint input. Theorem 3.1 supplies the legal
reduced canonical continuation, including all five labelled failures and
the separately retained absorbing status. The unitaries and Kraus
operations needed for this attainment are conditional services, not
hardware obtained from the original source law. This proves the claimed
minimum. $\square$

